1
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,280
Successful cell culture depends heavily on keeping the cells free from

2
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:12,840
microorganism contamination. This can be accomplished with proper knowledge of

3
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:17,220
sterile environments, working slowly and deliberately, and following all of the

4
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:21,360
guidelines for sterile technique. Always wear basic personal protective

5
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:26,040
equipment, or PPE, when working in the laboratory. Talk to the safety team at

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:31,520
your Institute for your required PPE. Also, remember to review the MSDS

7
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:36,440
information before working with any media or reagents. Wear closed-toed shoes

8
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:44,410
and clothes that cover your legs. Washing your hands before handling cells or

9
00:00:44,410 --> 00:00:49,730
performing culture work removes bacteria and microscopic dead skin particles.

10
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:56,250
Dead skin cells can be a potential source of contamination. A 70% ethanol wash kills

11
00:00:56,250 --> 00:01:01,050
microorganisms that could contaminate the cultures. Cleanliness is one of the most

12
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:05,850
important parts of sterile technique. Always clean the hood before and after

13
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:11,410
use. Spray alcohol should not be used in any area where a flame is being used due

14
00:01:11,410 --> 00:01:16,490
to the fire hazard. The outside of containers carry dust and contaminants.

15
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:21,090
Remember to clean each item placed in the hood with ethanol. You may choose to

16
00:01:21,090 --> 00:01:25,370
spray the item before placing it in the hood or immediately after, as demonstrated.

17
00:01:25,370 --> 00:01:29,330
As you clean the item and place it into the cell culture hood, put it in the

18
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:34,090
correct position. Enter the hood with your hands in a forward motion. Try not to

19
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:38,810
sweep across the front and disrupt the airflow. Proper setup of the cell culture

20
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:43,530
hood includes not overcrowding the work surface. Not only does this increase the

21
00:01:43,530 --> 00:01:47,450
risk of contamination through accidental touching, but it also interrupts the

22
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:51,890
airflow through the chamber, which will not maintain the sterile field. For the

23
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:55,570
same reason, it is important to keep the front sash in the lower position

24
00:01:55,570 --> 00:02:01,010
when working. The basic idea is to keep your media and reagents sterile by only

25
00:02:01,010 --> 00:02:06,290
touching them with sterile objects. Using sterile media, reagents, and supplies is

26
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:11,070
a big step in keeping your cultures free from contamination. Notice how the

27
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:16,250
cell culture hood is set up to make the following actions easier. The pipette aid

28
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:19,570
is on the right side, so you can easily control the pipetting in your

29
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:23,770
right hand. Reagents are in the center back, so you can easily open the

30
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:28,010
bottles and pipette from them. It is very important to have the setup in this

31
00:02:28,010 --> 00:02:32,730
manner, so you're not crossing your hands or supplies over top of sterile items.

32
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:37,370
We'll now demonstrate the basic technique of handling a pipette and a bottle of

33
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:41,970
medium while aseptically supplementing DMEM with non-essential amino acids.

34
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:46,210
When opening the pipette package, be sure the pipette does not touch anything

35
00:02:46,210 --> 00:02:51,250
non-sterile. Grasp the pipette high on the neck. Insert the pipette into the

36
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:55,890
pipette aid. Turn the desired measurement marks toward you and then discard the

37
00:02:55,890 --> 00:03:01,170
wrapper. Discard any pipette you accidentally contaminate. Only open your

38
00:03:01,170 --> 00:03:05,890
media, reagents, and supplies in the cell culture hood. Opening these items

39
00:03:05,890 --> 00:03:10,490
outside the sterile field will result in contamination. When holding the cap, it

40
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:15,170
is important not to touch the inside edge or you could contaminate it. Replace

41
00:03:15,170 --> 00:03:18,970
the cap as soon as possible. If you must set the cap down in order to free

42
00:03:18,970 --> 00:03:24,570
your hand, set it down with the interior surface facing down. When pipetting, try

43
00:03:24,570 --> 00:03:28,870
not to touch the pipette to anything non-sterile, particularly the outside of

44
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:33,650
containers or contamination could result. Each pipette should be used one

45
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:39,090
time and discarded or washed if using glass pipettes. Reusing pipettes by

46
00:03:39,090 --> 00:03:42,890
dipping directly into another media bottle or by leaving it standing in a

47
00:03:42,890 --> 00:03:48,210
media bottle increases your chances of spreading contamination. Gently mix the

48
00:03:48,210 --> 00:03:53,090
contents after supplementation. Labeling the bottle after supplementation is a good

49
00:03:53,090 --> 00:03:57,970
laboratory practice. Sometimes you'll need to transfer large volumes of liquid

50
00:03:57,970 --> 00:04:02,250
from one container to another. The best way to transfer is always to use a

51
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:07,490
sterile pipette, but with practice it is possible to aseptically pour instead.

52
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:11,650
You should pour quickly and deliberately using the front corner of

53
00:04:11,650 --> 00:04:15,330
the bottle to channel the liquid and improve the speed and accuracy of your

54
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:20,370
pour. Any spills should be wiped with ethanol immediately. When passaging cells

55
00:04:20,370 --> 00:04:25,330
or changing their medium, the basics of aseptic technique are the same. Only

56
00:04:25,330 --> 00:04:29,490
open the containers in the sterile field. Do not cross your arms or other

57
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:34,570
items over an open flask. Don't rush, but work at a good pace and with

58
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:39,250
deliberate motions. Do not reuse pipettes and do not use items that were

59
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:44,290
inadvertently contaminated. When finished, make sure everything is closed tightly

60
00:04:44,290 --> 00:04:49,050
before removing the cell culture hood. Anything opened outside the sterile field

61
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:53,850
will now be non-sterile and should not be used for cell culture work. Wipe down

62
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:59,570
the work surface with ethanol again and straighten up the hood before you leave.
